Protocols: set of rules defining how data should be communicated between devices over a network.

Standards: agreed-upon specification that decsribes how a protocol or technology should work.

TCP/IP Layers: Application layer -> the text of the letter, what you want to say
               Transport layer -> the intended recipient
               Internet layer -> the destination address of the recipient
               Data-link -> destination of the next hop
               Physical layer -> the infrastructure used

Application -> protocols for communication between application processes; create and interpret data. Protocols at this layer define message formats and rules for example
http/https, ftp/tftp, SMTP, IMAP, etc.

Transport -> provides end-to-end communication between application processes using port numbers. Protocols at this layer are TCP and UDP. At this layer, the data is called
segment (TCP) or datagram (UDP).

Internet -> provides end-to-end communication between hosts across network using IP adresses and routers. Protocols at this layer are IP and ICMP. At this layer, the data is
called packet.

Data-link -> provides hop-to-hop delivery within a local network using MAC addresses and switches. Protocols at this layer are Ethernet and wifi. At this layer, the data is
called a frame.

Physical -> sends bits as electrical, optical, or radio signals over the physical medium.


Payload: the data when we remove the header. So at layer 4, the payload is the application data (we remove the L4 header), at layer 3, it's the segment/datagram, at layer 2,
it's the packet, and at layer 1, it's the frame.
